title: Lukcau, 9th October, 1942
New-page: true
  
An order arrived and names were read. You were shackled, in pairs, to another man. You were led out to the prisoner transport on the Reichsbahn. A converted freight car with small, barred windows. 

The war economy needs warm bodies.

Buchenwald, 11th October, 1942.

There was bodies hanging when you arrived at the Appellplatz. 

You were taken, you were shaved, disinfected, forcibly tattooed with a number. You are a Zugang, you are twenty nine years old.

Now what is your life? You are in the stone quarry, you are labouring in your Kommando, it is tough work, it is unbearable work that breaks your depleted strength. Forty, fifty kilogram stones, carried up at a wooden staircase. Kapos, SS men, all beat you. 

Death feels inevitable. Your comrades are sick. Sometimes you see, presumably, Soviet men being dragged into a stable, you aren't sure what happens there. You aren't sure you want to know what happens.

Buchenwald is hell for a man to end his life in slavery. Twice a day, in the rain and snow, in wooden clogs and thin uniformed, for an hour, maybe two, the Appell. Men freeze to the ground, some men collapse, some of them die.  

You hunger, you waste, you have a nettle soup at midday, a black bread at night with a sliver of margarine or sausage maybe a few times a week. You tried to count your calories, maybe a thousand at best, usually less. How much were you using? 

Around you, the Muselmann, skeletal, wrapped in blanket, eyes vacant, unresponsive, you wonder how long it will take before you become this.

You were whipped, 25 lashes, by one of your comrades. You were tied, suspended by the wrists, till your shoulders dislocated. You hung there for six hours, you didn't do anything.

You were lucky to be alive, you had no hard feelings for the comrade who lashed you, such is life. You lashed your own later on, you had to so as to survive here.

You became a clerk. Your Kommando changed. You had friends, amongst the party, we continued fighting, just like at Luckau. You maintained a card index of each prisoner, alongside your comrades. Denouncing, liquidiating, protecting. 

You died, a few times. Somehow, your body always came back. You didn't. At least, you didn't feel like you did. 
  
